# Before adding a pattern that matches inside a source directory: ignoring a path
# keeps it out of tests/support/build.ts's source set, but not out of the mtime of
# the directory holding it, and every directory holding a source is read there. An
# untracked components/.DS_Store already reports the build stale naming the file;
# ignoring it makes the same run report `components/ gained, lost or renamed an
# entry`, which is harder to diagnose, not quieter. Patterns with no leading slash
# match at any depth, so this reaches further than the artifacts-at-the-root shape
# the list below happens to have today.
#
# `.DS_Store` below is exactly that pattern, added anyway: unlike an arbitrary
# developer artifact, it is one well-known macOS Finder file with no meaningful
# content, so a degraded staleness message naming the directory instead of the
# file is a cost worth paying to stop it from piling up untracked wherever
# Finder opens.

# Local scratch that is not this project's source. These are ignored for a
# second reason beyond keeping `git status` readable, and it is the load-bearing
# one: **Tailwind v4's automatic source detection skips gitignored paths**, so an
# untracked-but-unignored directory of HTML is scanned as source and its class
# names ship. Measured on `.design/` (design mocks, one of them 2.3 MB): the
# popup CSS built 105,485 B with it present and 45,818 B with it listed here —
# the directory still on disk, only the ignore line added. 45,818 is what CI
# builds, so the difference was the whole gap between a local build and the
# released one.
.superpowers/
.design/
.zcode/
.DS_Store
node_modules
.output/
.wxt/
.worktrees/
test-results/
# @vitest/coverage-v8 is installed, so `--coverage` is one flag away. Untracked
# and unignored, its output would land in tests/support/build.ts's source set
# and report both builds stale — every build-reading test failing with a message
# about staleness rather than about coverage. A root-level artifact, like the
# rest of this list, which is what makes it safe: newestSource() excludes the
# repo root's own mtime.
coverage/
playwright-report/
blob-report/
playwright/.cache/

# npm is not this project's package manager, and a stray `npm install` writing
# one of these is easy to commit without noticing. pnpm-lock.yaml is the lockfile.
package-lock.json
yarn.lock

# The Chrome Web Store's verified-upload signing key, and anything packed with
# it. `scripts/pack-crx.mjs` keeps the key in 1Password and writes it only to a
# 0600 file inside a temp directory it removes on every exit path, so neither
# pattern should ever have anything to match — which is the point: a key that
# reaches this tree at all is a mistake, and `git add -A` must not be what
# discovers it. Do not read that as making these patterns redundant: the cleanup
# was written as a `try/finally` first and did not run at all on the script's own
# refusals, which is the kind of thing this list is the backstop for. These do
# match at any depth, against the note at the top of this file; a confusing
# staleness message is the cheaper failure of the two.
*.pem
*.crx

# `wxt submit init` writes the Firefox Add-ons API key and secret into
# `.env.submit`, and the unlisted release path writes the signed Firefox package
# beside the zips. Neither should ever be what `git add -A` discovers. The
# credentials live in 1Password (`Firefox AMO Token`) and in the `firefox-amo`
# environment; `scripts/amo-submit.mjs` refuses to run while `.env.submit`
# exists, because `wxt submit` would read it. See docs/store/amo/checklist.md.
.env.submit
*.xpi
